# KNOWN GAP — STRUCT CONSTRUCTOR ARGUMENTS ARE NOT TYPE-CHECKED AGAINST THE
# FIELD TYPE. `gg check` accepts, and the payload shape decides whether you get
# a wrong value or a SIGSEGV.
#
#     struct Sink:
#         String s
#
#     Sink(42)                  -> gg check rc 0, runs rc 0, prints EMPTY
#     Sink(Some(mk(...)))       -> gg check rc 0, runs rc 139 SIGSEGV (both lanes)
#
# THE CONSTRUCTOR ARGUMENT POSITION IS THE DISCRIMINATOR. Every other position
# rejects the same mismatch correctly:
#
#     void take(String s): ...
#     take(42)                  -> gg check rc 1, REJECTED   (plain call)
#     String v = 42             -> gg check rc 1, REJECTED   (named local)
#
# So this is not a missing rule in general -- it is a rule with no SUBJECT at
# the ctor-argument position (Core #15e Q4, a category error). Widening the
# call-argument rule does not reach it; the position needs its own check.
#
# ⇒ Core #8/#10: a wrong-ACCEPT that reaches codegen. `gg check` does not
# reach lowering, so nothing downstream re-checks it.
#
# ⚠ USE A HEAP-FORCED PAYLOAD FOR THE CRASHING CELL (`mk(...)`, never a
# literal). With a literal the second cell may not fault, and the fixture
# becomes a false negative.
#
# INTENDED (asserted): BOTH spellings are REJECTED at check time with a type
# mismatch naming the field and the argument type. Neither should reach
# codegen.
# Measured 2026-08-27 by R45 Track D brief-review pass 4; orchestrator-verified
# with all four positions run side by side.

String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

struct Sink:
    String s

void main():
    Sink a = Sink(Some(mk("aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa", "bbbbbbbbbbbbbbbb")))
    print(a.s)
